ir. So maybe not.\n\nIf they play W1, they lose that tile, but the W2, W3 can form a chow if they have a W4, but they don't. So maybe not helpful.\n\nIf they play W5, they have W6, W7 left. But then they can form a chow of W5-6-7 if they have W5. But since they played W5, they don't. So not helpful.\n\nIf they play W7, they have W5, W6 left. Need W4 to form a chow. Not present.\n\nIf they play T4, they have two left. Not helpful for pung. But maybe they can form a chow with T4 and others? Not sure.\n\nIf they play B9, they have B7 and B3 left. Not helpful.\n\nIf they play T7, they have T3 and T4 (three). Maybe a chow of T3-4-5? No T5. Not helpful.\n\nIf they play B7, they have B3 and B9. Not helpful.\n\nIf they play W2, they have W1 and W3. Need W2 to form a chow. But they played it. So no.\n\nIf they play W3, they have W1 and W2 left. Need W3 to form a chow. But they played it. So no.\n\nIf they play W6, they have W5 and W7. Need W6 to form a chow. But they played it. So no.\n\nSo the only viable options seem to be playing T3 or T4. Between those, playing T3 allows keeping the T4 pung. Even though the remaining tiles don't form a fourth meld, maybe the user can draw a tile that completes it. For example, if they play T3, then draw a T4, they can form a kong. Or maybe draw a tile that completes a chow in another suit.\n\nAlternatively, playing T4 would break the pung, but maybe allow for a different pattern. But I don't see a better pattern.\n\nGiven that, the best move is to play T3. That keeps the T4 pung intact, which is a good component for scoring. The user can then hope to draw a tile that completes the fourth meld or pair.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing T3 allows maintaining the potential for a pung of T4 and sets up two chows in the W suit. This keeps the hand flexible for forming All Chows or other patterns.\nReason: By discarding T3, the hand retains three T4s (a potential pung) and two chows (W1-3 and W5-7). This setup maximizes the chance to form four chows and a pair, which is an 8-point pattern. Discarding T3 also avoids losing a tile that isn't part of a pung or chow.\nAnswer: Play T3"}
