 Let's see:\n\nPossible chows:\n\n1. W1-2-3 (Characters)\n2. W5-6-7 (Characters)\n3. T4-5-6 (Bamboo) if I have T4, T5, T6. But I have T4 (two), T5 (one), but no T6. So can't form.\n4. B5-6-7 (Dots) if I have B5, B6, B7. I have B5 (two), B7 (two), but no B6. So can't form.\n\nSo only two chows. Not enough. So maybe this isn't feasible.\n\nHmm. Maybe I need to discard a tile that allows me to form a third chow. Let's see. If I can get a T6, then T4-5-6. But I don't have T6. Or a B6 for B5-6-7. Unshown B6 has 2 left. Maybe if I discard a tile, I can draw a B6 or T6. But that's uncertain.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard a tile that's not part of any chow. Like T7. Then, if I can draw a T6, I can form T4-5-6. But I need to keep the T5. So if I discard T7, I still have T4 and T5. If I draw T6, then I can form that chow. Similarly, if I discard T5, I lose the possibility of T4-5-6. So better to discard T7.\n\nSo the best move might be to discard T7, keeping the possibility of forming more chows if I can draw the needed tiles. Also, T7 is not part of any existing chow, so discarding it doesn't break anything. That way, I can keep the W chows and T4 pair, and maybe form another chow later.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding T7 is the optimal move as it preserves potential chows in Characters and Dots while maintaining the T4 pair.  \nReason: T7 is not part of any current chow and discarding it allows flexibility to form additional chows (e.g., T4-5-6 if T6 is drawn) or maintain the concealed hand structure for scoring.  \nAnswer: Play T7"}
