nclude a Chi of T3-T4-T5. The user's hand has W2, W3x2, W5, W6, W7, W8, B5, B7, B9, J2. If they discard W8, they remove a tile that could be part of a chow (W6-7-8). But they have W6, W7, W8. If they discard W8, they lose that chow. So maybe better to keep W8 for the chow.\n\nSo, the user has W6, W7, W8. If they discard W8, they can't form the W6-7-8 chow. So it's better to keep W8. Therefore, the safer tile to discard is W2.\n\nDiscarding W2: the user has W2 in their hand. If they discard it, it's possible that the next player could take it for a Chi if they have W1 and W3. But the next player's melds are B2, so they might not be targeting W2. The previous player has Peng W2, so they already have three. So discarding W2 is safe. And it doesn't affect any potential chows in the user's hand.\n\nTherefore, the best action is to play W2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The optimal move is to discard W2 as it is a single tile not part of any potential melds in the current hand and is less likely to be claimed by other players.]\nReason: [Discarding W2 preserves key tiles for potential chows (W5-6-7 and W6-7-8) and avoids disrupting a possible Pung of W3. Other tiles like W8 are part of a potential chow and should be retained.]\nAnswer: Play W2"}
