orm another Chow. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a Pung. For example, if I have two W3s, and can get another, but there's only one left. But I have two already. If I discard a W3, maybe someone else could take it. \n\nWait, the valid actions are to Play W8, B3, W5, B9, W7, B5, J2, B7, W6, W3. \n\nIf I play W8, I discard it. If someone else takes it for a Chi or Peng. But if I play W8, I might lose the chance to form a Chow. \n\nAlternatively, if I play W3, I have two, so playing one reduces to one. \n\nWait, maybe I should hold onto the W3s for a pair. Since the pair is part of the All Chows. \n\nLet me think about the points. If I can form four Chows and a pair, All Chows gives 2 points. If I can also have a Fully Concealed Hand, but I already have an exposed Chi. So that's not possible. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a Mixed Straight (8 points) if I have all three suits. But I don't have T or B tiles for that. \n\nLooking at the Dots (B) tiles: B3, B5, B7, B9. Not helpful for Chows. \n\nThe J2 is a Green Dragon. If I can form a Pung, but I only have one. \n\nMaybe the best way is to form as many Chows as possible. \n\nIf I can form W5-6-7 and W6-7-8, that's two Chows. Plus the existing T3-4-5. That's three Chows. Plus the pair of W3. Then I need one more Chow. \n\nBut where? Maybe if I can form another Chow in T or B. \n\nBut I don't have T or B tiles for that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a Pung of W3. If I have two, and there's one left. If I can get a third W3, but there's only one left. \n\nBut the unshown tiles say W3 has 1 left. So if I discard a W3, and someone else takes it, I can't form a Pung. \n\nAlternatively, if I keep the W3s, maybe I can form a Pung if I get another. But since there's only one left, it's risky. \n\nWait, the valid actions include playing W3. If I play W3, I discard it. But if I keep it, maybe I can use it for a Pung. \n\nBut given that there's only one W3 left, if I discard one, I can't form a Pung. \n\nSo maybe it'